eadership (crew cooperation), but not open to new ideas (adaptability). D has responsibility, problem solving, concentration, but weak leadership and arrogance.\n\nA has quick decisions, attention, concern for others (crew cooperation), but communication difficulty and egocentric may affect crew.\n\nGiven the panel, we need to converge. Perhaps we can argue that Candidate C's proactive safety awareness (anticipate dangerous situations) and leadership outweigh his show-off and lack of openness, and that these can be mitigated. Or we could argue B's stress handling and conscientiousness are more critical, and his interpersonal issues can be mitigated. Or we could argue D's responsibility and problem solving are crucial.\n\nWe need to make a focused contribution, referencing prior discussion. Perhaps we can propose that we need to weigh crew cooperation vs technical competence. We could shift our vote to Candidate C, aligning with Agent 2, to move towards consensus. Or we could propose a tie-breaker: we need more info on reliability, but lacking that, we choose based on strongest safety-critical traits. Candidate C's anticipation of dangerous situations is unique and directly addresses safety. So we could argue for C.\n\nThus as Agent 3, we can change our vote to Candidate C, providing reasoning: C's anticipation, technical, leadership outweigh his show-off and lack of openness, which can be mitigated. Also note that all candidates lack reliability data, but C's proactive safety is critical.\n\nWe must include explicit facts: anticipate dangerous situations, sees complex connections, excellent spatial vision, very good leadership qualities, regarded as show\u2011off, not open to new ideas.\n\nWe also need to reference prior discussion: mention that Agent 2 highlighted C, Agent 1 argued B, Agent 3 earlier argued A, but after considering D's issues, we think C is best.\n\nThus produce public message and metadata JSON with vote Candidate C.\n\nProceed."}
